You're absolutely on the right track, David=E2=80=94and your memory serves =
you well. While **XML doesn't natively support hyperlinking like HTML does*=
*, there are a couple of nuanced ways to weave in relational context betwee=
n documents:

---

### =F0=9F=A7=B7 1. **Using a Processing Instruction (PI)**
This is not machine-enforced, but it=E2=80=99s a common archival convention=
 to guide consumers of XML:

```xml
&lt;?see-also href=3D"G713QM_Gestalt_Spec.md" ?&gt;
```

This acts as a hint to parsers or humans reading the file but isn=E2=80=99t=
 rendered or followed like HTML. It=E2=80=99s purely informational.

---

### =F0=9F=94=97 2. **Embedding an RDF-style Element or Custom Tag**
If you=E2=80=99re building a spec-aware schema (e.g. ISO archive), you can =
create a wrapper tag:

```xml
&lt;relatedDocumentation&gt;
  &lt;markdownSpec href=3D"G713QM_Gestalt_Spec.md" /&gt;
&lt;/relatedDocumentation&gt;
```

Or:

```xml
&lt;documentationLink&gt;./G713QM_Gestalt_Spec.md&lt;/documentationLink&gt;
```

This makes the relationship clear and machine-readable, especially if you e=
ver use XSLT or XML parsers down the line.

---

### =F0=9F=93=84 3. **XLink and Namespacing (Advanced)**
If you want strict adherence to linked-resource specs, you could use **XLin=
k**:

```xml
&lt;link xmlns:xlink=3D"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
      xlink:type=3D"simple"
      xlink:href=3D"G713QM_Gestalt_Spec.md"
      xlink:title=3D"Markdown Archive Document"/&gt;
```

It=E2=80=99s overkill for local use, but if you ever export to RDF/XML, it=
=E2=80=99s schema-compliant.

---

### Bottom Line:
You can=E2=80=99t make the link *clickable* inside plain XML without an ext=
ernal processor or viewer that supports it (like a web-based renderer), but=
 **you absolutely can encode the reference in a standard way for documentat=
ion, traceability, or downstream automation.**

