## Summary

During archival cleanup of a deconstructed `.wim` capture directory, a bulk=
 right-click rename using `...` via Windows Explorer was applied to ~20 `Wi=
nSxS` subfolders. This unintentionally truncated *only the middle section* =
of each folder name, preserving the prefix (`amd64_</span></span><span init=
ial=3D"start" animate=3D"end" variants=3D"[object Object]" custom=3D"0">...=
</span><span initial=3D"start" animate=3D"end" variants=3D"[object Object]"=
 custom=3D"0"><span class=3D"hljs-code">`) and suffix (`</span></span><span=
 initial=3D"start" animate=3D"end" variants=3D"[object Object]" custom=3D"0=
">none</span><span initial=3D"start" animate=3D"end" variants=3D"[object Ob=
ject]" custom=3D"0"><span class=3D"hljs-emphasis">_&lt;hash&gt;`) while sil=
ently discarding the namespace core.

&gt; =E2=80=9CLike if Borges wrote a filesystem=E2=80=94stripped of content=
, but still locatable by its index.=E2=80=9D

---

## Trigger Chain

1. User selected multiple deeply-nested folders in `Windows\WinSxS`
2. Applied right-click rename using `...`
3. Explorer auto-truncated each name, preserving only start+end structure
4. Folder identities lost; future delete/overwrite operations began to fail
5. Ghost reparse points became inaccessible

---

## Resolution

- Original folder identities recovered via prior metadata log:
  - `=3D=3D=3D DLL Force-Cleanup Log =3D=3D=3D` provided accurate paths
- Scripted `ren` operations mapped hash suffixes to full canonical names
- Folder names restored deterministically
- Filesystem reparse logic successfully bypassed during later cleanup

---

## Lessons Learned

- <span class=3D"hljs-strong">**Explorer rename + wildcard is filesystem ro=
ulette**</span>
- `WinSxS` metadata names carry semantic payload=E2=80=94not just storage s=
tructure
- Logging cleanup operations <span class=3D"hljs-strong">**before**</span> =
execution is a critical failsafe
- Ghost reparse points can be traced back to malformed `.wim` composition

---

## Follow-Up

- `restore-folder-names.cmd` added to recovery toolkit =20
- Case indexed in `timeline.md` as `Case 045` =20
- Ghost cleanup methodology archived for future regression tooling

