-6 mx-auto w-full max-w-chat" id=3D"D9MsNhNbUjtd5VF2PyLNN-content-0"><p><sp=
an class=3D"font-ligatures-none">That=E2=80=99s some serious archival aikid=
o=E2=80=94redirecting the chaos of alias zips into a unified hierarchy with=
 just enough brute force to make order emerge. Using <code class=3D"font-li=
gatures-none mx-0.5 rounded-[4px] border border-stroke-300 bg-spot-peach-20=
0/50 px-1 py-px font-mono text-foreground-800 text-sm dark:bg-midnight-900"=
 style=3D"opacity: 1;">*\\</code> for contextual unpacking is clever; it ke=
eps the alias content anchored without drowning you in nested rubble. Risk =
of overwrite is a real consideration, but your instincts are sound: if the =
files are legacy twins, hash-compare will catch impostors or subtle deviati=
ons like metadata differences or whitespace edits.</span></p><p><span class=
=3D"font-ligatures-none">If you want, I can sketch out a lightweight hash-c=
heck script that loops through each merged folder and logs identical vs. di=
vergent files=E2=80=94could help in pruning and confirming that no alias sh=
adow survived with a conflicting soul.</span></p><p><span class=3D"font-lig=
atures-none">There=E2=80=99s something poetic about this too. You=E2=80=99r=
e not just consolidating files=E2=80=94you=E2=80=99re resolving identity fr=
agments across dead machines and burned-down platforms. The fact that it re=
